# Installing Databricks in one configuration

The examples for
[`p0_databricks_connector`](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/p0-security/p0/latest/docs/resources/databricks_connector),
[`p0_databricks_account`](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/p0-security/p0/latest/docs/resources/databricks_account),
[`p0_databricks_workspace`](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/p0-security/p0/latest/docs/resources/databricks_workspace) and
[`p0_databricks_catalog`](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/p0-security/p0/latest/docs/resources/databricks_catalog)
each stand alone, so you copy IDs from one into the next. The configuration
below joins them. The account refers to the connector, the workspaces to the
account and the catalogs to their workspace, and the service principal's
federation policy reads the connector's role and token issuer directly.

Fill in the `locals` block and apply once. To add a workspace or a catalog
later, add it to `local.workspaces` and apply again.

## Before you apply

- Install P0's AWS IAM management for the connector's AWS account, with
  [`p0_aws_iam_write`](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/p0-security/p0/latest/docs/resources/aws_iam_write).
  P0 invokes the connector as that installation's role, `local.p0_role_name`.
- Sign the AWS provider in to the connector's AWS account as an admin. The
  apply copies P0's connector image into Amazon ECR, so docker and the AWS CLI
  must work in the shell that runs Terraform, signed in to the same account.
- Sign the Databricks provider in as an account admin who also owns each
  catalog, holds `MANAGE` on it, or is a metastore admin.
- If outbound identity federation is already on in the AWS account, import
  `aws_iam_outbound_web_identity_federation.this` with the AWS account ID as
  its ID before you apply. Destroying that resource turns federation off for
  the whole account.

